Or the ones that have faster systems that don't have to type ahead.  When
playing Nethack on an XT (something I occasionally endure), typeahead has
killed me MUCH more often than when I have played on 286 & 386 machines.  I
can't tell you how many times that gelatinous cube or floating eye got me
because my system couldn't keep up with me.  I do generally use the "run"
keys, but there are times when you just have to get around that corner or
down that corridor filled with items (There are several objects here
-more-).  On levels that have large shops or ghosts or worst (for game
speed) of all, the dreaded orc horde, the XT processor just can't keep up
at all.  I have been on many a level where walking across a 3x5 room takes
upwards of 5 seconds!  Sorry guys, but I don't always have that kind of
patience.  (It is something I aspire to though ... :) ).

Well, I guess what I'm trying to say is this:
	1. Yes, you should be made to pay for your mistakes -- to a point.
	2. I don't think your score should suffer because of the
		capabilities of the system you are using.
	3. IMHO item 2 is more important (to me) than item 1.

By the way, this is somewhat less of a problem, but still an issue when I
play on my 1200 baud modem.  I don't get the "large level syndrome" when
playing on a minicomputer, but just walking around on ANY level at 1200
baud can be enough to make me type ahead a bit.

